CH = dict(num=5, title="Describing Motion Around Us", tag="Physics", theme="Distance, displacement, speed, velocity, acceleration, graphs, kinematic equations, circular motion",
intro="To study the huge variety of motion in nature, physics begins with idealised forms: **linear** (straight line), **circular** and **oscillatory**. This chapter describes straight-line motion precisely with **position, displacement, average speed, average velocity and average acceleration**, shows how to read and draw **position–time and velocity–time graphs**, derives the **three kinematic equations**, and ends with **uniform circular motion**.",
big_questions=["How much distance should we keep from the truck ahead to avoid a collision if it brakes suddenly?", "Does this distance depend on the speed at which we are moving?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "5.1 Describing Position and Motion"),
("ul", ["Position needs a fixed **reference point (origin O)**, the **distance** from it and the **direction**. In a straight line, direction is shown by signs: right of O is **positive (+)**, left is **negative (−)**.",
 "An object is **in motion** if its position relative to the reference point changes with time; it is **at rest** if it does not. (Hence an object on Earth is at rest relative to the ground but moving relative to the Sun.)",
 "An **instant** is a single clock reading; a **time interval** is the duration between two instants — not the same thing.",
 "We choose the origin and positive direction for convenience, but must not change them while solving a problem (e.g. origin at ground in a thrown-ball problem; origin at the release point and downward positive for a dropped object)."]),

("h2", "5.2 Distance and Displacement"),
("table", ["", "Distance travelled", "Displacement"], [
 ["Meaning", "Total length of path travelled", "Net change in position between two instants (straight-line gap from start to end)"],
 ["Quantity type", "Scalar — magnitude only", "Vector — magnitude and direction"],
 ["Can be zero?", "Only if no motion at all", "Yes, even after a long trip (return to start)"],
 ["Relation", "Always ≥ magnitude of displacement; equal when motion is in one direction without turning back", ""],
 ["SI unit", "metre (m)", "metre (m)"]]),
("ul", ["**Athlete example:** O → A (100 m) → back to B (40 m mark). Total distance = 100 + 60 = **160 m**; displacement = **40 m** in the positive direction.",
 "Ball thrown up and returning to O: at the highest point (B = 60 cm) distance = displacement = 60 cm; when back at O, distance 120 cm, displacement 0. So the magnitude of displacement is **less than or equal to** the distance, never greater.",
 "Fuel used depends on **distance travelled**, not displacement. A ball rolling down a curved track is not in straight-line motion; at points where it has not turned back, distance and magnitude of displacement are equal along the path only if the path is straight."]),
("note", "REMEMBER", "Scalars (distance, speed, time) need only a number with unit; **vectors** (displacement, velocity, acceleration) need magnitude and direction."),

("h2", "5.3 Average Speed and Average Velocity"),
("note", "FORMULA", "Average speed = total distance travelled ÷ time interval.   Average velocity v<sub>av</sub> = displacement ÷ time interval = s ÷ t.   SI unit m s<sup>−1</sup> (also km h<sup>−1</sup>; 1 km h<sup>−1</sup> = 5/18 m s<sup>−1</sup>)."),
("table", ["", "Average speed", "Average velocity"], [
 ["Based on", "Distance", "Displacement"], ["Direction", "None (scalar)", "Same as displacement; sign + or − in a line (vector)"], ["Can be zero?", "No, if the body has moved", "Yes (if it returns to start)"]]),
("ul", ["Equal distances in equal time intervals (for every choice of interval) → **uniform motion** (constant speed). Unequal distances in equal intervals → **non-uniform motion** (speed increasing, decreasing or both).",
 "Magnitude of average velocity = average speed **only when motion is in one direction** without turning back.",
 "Velocity is the **average rate of change of position** with time. 'Velocity' in this chapter means velocity at an instant (**instantaneous velocity**, approached as the time interval becomes infinitesimally small). A speedometer shows its magnitude; the tyres’ direction gives its direction."]),
("table", ["Example", "Working", "Answer"], [
 ["Two postmen 210 yojanas apart walking towards each other at 9 and 5 yojanas/day (Ganitakaumudi, 14th c.)", "Combined 14 yojanas/day; 210 ÷ 14", "15 days (135 and 75 yojanas)"],
 ["Sarang swims to the far end and back in 50 s in a 25 m pool", "Distance 50 m; displacement 0", "Speed 1 m s<sup>−1</sup>; velocity 0"],
 ["Drive 200 km north in 3 h, then 200 km south in 2 h", "Distance 400 km, time 5 h; displacement 0", "Speed 80 km h<sup>−1</sup>; velocity 0"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "Magnitude of average velocity is **zero but average speed non-zero** whenever the object returns to its starting point (a round trip, a full lap)."),

("h2", "5.4 Average Acceleration"),
("note", "FORMULA", "a = (final velocity − initial velocity) ÷ time interval = (v − u) ÷ (t<sub>2</sub> − t<sub>1</sub>).   SI unit **m s<sup>−2</sup>**."),
("ul", ["Acceleration is a vector. In a straight line: if the **magnitude of velocity increases**, acceleration is in the direction of velocity (+); if it **decreases** (retardation/deceleration), acceleration is **opposite** to velocity (−).",
 "Acceleration arises from a change in the **magnitude** of velocity, its **direction**, or both.",
 "Acceleration depends on **how quickly velocity changes**, not on how fast the object moves: a bus at a steady 100 km h<sup>−1</sup> has zero acceleration.",
 "If velocity changes by equal amounts in equal intervals of time, acceleration is **constant**. All treatment here assumes constant acceleration. Acceleration at an instant is called instantaneous acceleration."]),
("table", ["Example", "Working", "Answer"], [
 ["Bus speeds up from 36 km h<sup>−1</sup> (10 m s<sup>−1</sup>) to 54 km h<sup>−1</sup> (15 m s<sup>−1</sup>) in 10 s", "(15 − 10) ÷ 10", "+0.5 m s<sup>−2</sup> (along velocity)"],
 ["Same bus brakes from 15 m s<sup>−1</sup> to rest in 5 s", "(0 − 15) ÷ 5", "−3 m s<sup>−2</sup> (opposite to velocity)"],
 ["Dropped object: velocities 0, 9.8, 19.6, 29.4, 39.2 m s<sup>−1</sup> at 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 s", "Each second change = 9.8 m s<sup>−1</sup>", "Constant **g = 9.8 m s<sup>−2</sup>**, downward"]]),
("note", "REMEMBER", "To convert km h<sup>−1</sup> to m s<sup>−1</sup> multiply by 1000/3600 = 5/18 (36 km h<sup>−1</sup> = 10 m s<sup>−1</sup>)."),

("h2", "5.5 Graphical Representation of Motion"),
("ul", ["Graphs show how position, velocity or acceleration depends on time. A graph is **not a route map**; it shows how the quantity changes with time.",
 "For motion in a straight line in one direction, distance = magnitude of displacement and speed = magnitude of velocity; if position is zero at t = 0, the position–time graph equals the distance–time graph.",
 "**Plotting:** draw perpendicular x- and y-axes meeting at the origin; time on the x-axis, the other quantity on the y-axis; choose a convenient scale (e.g. 5 divisions = 1 s; 5 divisions = 20 m); mark each pair of values; join the points (straight line or smooth curve). Example data: 0, 20, 40, 60, 80, 100, 120 m at 0–6 s gives a straight line; 0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36 m at 0, 2,…, 12 s gives a curve."]),
("h3", "Position–time graph"),
("table", ["Shape of graph", "Meaning"], [
 ["Straight line sloping up", "Constant velocity (equal displacements in equal times)"],
 ["Curve (steepening)", "Changing velocity — accelerated motion"],
 ["Line parallel to time axis", "Object at **rest** (position unchanged)"],
 ["Steeper line (greater slope)", "Greater velocity (B steeper than A → v<sub>B</sub> > v<sub>A</sub>)"]]),
("note", "FORMULA", "**Slope** of a position–time graph = change in position ÷ change in time = **velocity**. Example: from (2 s, 40 m) to (4 s, 80 m): (80 − 40) ÷ (4 − 2) = **20 m s<sup>−1</sup>**. Parallel lines on a position–time graph mean equal velocities."),
("h3", "Velocity–time graph"),
("table", ["Shape of graph", "Motion", "Acceleration"], [
 ["Horizontal line", "Constant velocity", "Zero"],
 ["Straight line rising", "Velocity increases uniformly", "Constant, along velocity (+)"],
 ["Straight line falling", "Velocity decreases uniformly", "Constant, opposite to velocity (−)"]]),
("ul", ["**Slope** of a velocity–time graph = (v − u) ÷ (t<sub>2</sub> − t<sub>1</sub>) = **acceleration**. Rising car: (10 − 5) ÷ (20 − 10) = **+0.5 m s<sup>−2</sup>**; falling car: **−0.5 m s<sup>−2</sup>**.",
 "**Area** between the velocity–time graph and the time axis = **displacement**. Constant velocity: 20 m s<sup>−1</sup> × 6 s = **120 m** (rectangle). Uniform acceleration: area = rectangle + triangle, e.g. between 10 s and 20 s: (5 × 10) + ½ × 10 × 5 = 50 + 25 = **75 m**.",
 "Total displacement over several phases = sum of areas of each phase (rectangle for constant velocity, triangle/trapezium for changing velocity)."]),
("table", ["From the graph", "Quantity", "How to find"], [["Position–time", "Velocity", "Slope"], ["Velocity–time", "Acceleration", "Slope"], ["Velocity–time", "Displacement", "Area under line"]]),

("h2", "5.6 Kinematic Equations (Constant Acceleration, Straight Line)"),
("note", "FORMULA", "**(1) v = u + at**     **(2) s = ut + ½ at²**     **(3) v² = u² + 2as**<br>u = initial velocity, v = final velocity, a = constant acceleration, t = time, s = displacement."),
("ul", ["**Derivation of (1):** from a = (v − u) ÷ t.",
 "**Derivation of (2):** displacement = area under the v–t graph = rectangle (u × t) + triangle (½ × t × (v − u)); put v − u = at → s = ut + ½ at².",
 "**Derivation of (3):** from (1), t = (v − u)/a; substitute in (2) and simplify → v² = u² + 2as.",
 "Two further forms: s = vt − ½ at² and s = ½ (u + v) t (the area of a trapezium).",
 "Valid **only for constant acceleration**. Signs of u, v, a, s show direction; for motion in one direction, distance = |s| and speed = |v|.",
 "A body starting from rest has u = 0; a body that stops has v = 0; a retarding body has negative a."]),
("table", ["Problem", "Working", "Answer"], [
 ["Brakes give a = −4 m s<sup>−2</sup>; stopping distance at 54 km h<sup>−1</sup> (15 m s<sup>−1</sup>) and 108 km h<sup>−1</sup> (30 m s<sup>−1</sup>)", "0 = u² − 8s → s = u²/8", "28.1 m and 112.5 m (doubling speed quadruples distance)"],
 ["Car from rest to 24 m s<sup>−1</sup> in 6 s", "a = 24/6; s = ½ × 4 × 36", "a = 4 m s<sup>−2</sup>; s = 72 m"],
 ["Bike u = 28 m s<sup>−1</sup> stops in 98 m", "0 = 784 + 2a(98) → a; t = 28/|a|", "a = −4 m s<sup>−2</sup>; t = 7 s"],
 ["Truck slows from 54 to 36 km h<sup>−1</sup> in 36 s", "u = 15, v = 10; s = ½ (15 + 10) × 36", "450 m"],
 ["Car: 0→20 m s<sup>−1</sup> in 5 s; 20 m s<sup>−1</sup> for 10 s; stops in 6 s", "50 m + 200 m + 60 m (areas)", "310 m"],
 ["Bus at 10 m s<sup>−1</sup>; obstacle 30 m ahead; 0.5 s reaction; a = −2.5 m s<sup>−2</sup>", "Reaction 5 m + braking 100/5 = 20 m = 25 m", "Stops 5 m short — safe"],
 ["Car 6 m s<sup>−1</sup> for 120 s then a = 1 m s<sup>−2</sup> for 6 s", "720 m + (6 × 6 + ½ × 36)", "774 m"]]),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Safe following distance:** stopping distance depends on speed when brakes are applied, road surface (wet/dry), braking capacity (retardation), tyre condition and the driver’s **reaction time**. It rises with the square of speed, so keep a larger gap at higher speed. V2V communication technology warns drivers of collisions."),

("h2", "5.7 Motion in a Plane and Uniform Circular Motion"),
("ul", ["Motion in a plane (two dimensions): overtaking vehicle, a kicked ball’s path, a satellite in circular orbit. Motion in space (three dimensions): a car climbing a mountain road, a bird flying.",
 "**Circular motion:** motion along a circle. For a child on a merry-go-round moving from A to C: distance is the arc ABC, displacement the straight chord AC — unequal.",
 "In **one revolution** (period T, radius R): distance = circumference = **2πR**; displacement = **0**. So average speed = **2πR ÷ T** while average velocity over T is **0**.",
 "**Uniform circular motion:** motion in a circle at **constant speed**. Speed is constant but the **direction of velocity changes continuously** (imagine a runner on a rectangular, then hexagonal, then circular track: more sides → turns more frequent → circle).",
 "Marble-in-ring activity: when the ring is lifted, the marble moves off in a **straight line** along the **tangent** at the point of release — the velocity at any point is along the tangent to the circle in the direction of motion.",
 "Since velocity changes direction, uniform circular motion is **accelerated motion** even at constant speed. Everyday talk limits ‘acceleration’ to speed changes, but a change of direction alone also means acceleration.",
 "Uniform circular motion is an **idealised model** (real motion rarely has perfectly constant speed and path) but is the base for planets revolving round the Sun and vehicles taking circular turns.",
 "Minute hand tip (7 cm) from 6:00 to 7:30 pm: 1.5 revolutions → distance = 1.5 × 2π × 7 ≈ **66 cm**; displacement = diameter = **14 cm**; speed ≈ 66/5400 ≈ 0.012 cm s<sup>−1</sup>; velocity ≈ 14/5400 ≈ 0.0026 cm s<sup>−1</sup>."]),

("qa", [
 ("Distinguish between distance and displacement.", "Distance: total path length, scalar. Displacement: shortest straight-line change of position from start to end, vector; can be zero while distance is not."),
 ("When are average speed and magnitude of average velocity equal?", "When the motion is in a straight line without turning back (one direction)."),
 ("Give the SI units and formulae of average velocity and acceleration.", "v = s/t in m s<sup>−1</sup>; a = (v − u)/t in m s<sup>−2</sup>."),
 ("Can an object with constant speed be accelerating? Example?", "Yes. A body in uniform circular motion has constant speed but changing direction of velocity."),
 ("What does the slope of position–time and velocity–time graphs give?", "Velocity and acceleration respectively."),
 ("What does the area under a velocity–time graph give?", "Displacement in that time interval."),
 ("What does a straight line parallel to the time axis mean in a (i) position–time (ii) velocity–time graph?", "(i) Object at rest; (ii) constant velocity, zero acceleration."),
 ("Write the three kinematic equations. When are they valid?", "v = u + at; s = ut + ½at²; v² = u² + 2as; only for constant acceleration in a straight line."),
 ("Why does stopping distance increase greatly with speed?", "From v² = u² + 2as, s = u²/2|a|, so doubling u gives four times s (28.1 m → 112.5 m)."),
 ("Define uniform circular motion. What is the direction of velocity?", "Motion in a circle with constant speed; velocity is along the tangent to the circle at that point."),
 ("A round trip gives zero average velocity. Why?", "Displacement is zero as the object returns to the starting point."),
 ("Derive s = ut + ½at² from a v–t graph.", "Area of rectangle (ut) + triangle (½ t (v − u)), and v − u = at.")]),
],
takeaways=["Distance is scalar (path length); displacement is a vector (net change in position) and its magnitude never exceeds distance.",
 "Average velocity = displacement ÷ time; average acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time (m s<sup>−2</sup>); acceleration reflects how quickly velocity changes, not how fast the body moves.",
 "Position–time slope = velocity; velocity–time slope = acceleration; area under velocity–time graph = displacement.",
 "For constant acceleration: v = u + at, s = ut + ½at², v² = u² + 2as.",
 "Stopping distance grows with the square of speed and with reaction time — keep a safe gap.",
 "In uniform circular motion speed is constant, velocity is tangential and changing, so it is accelerated; one revolution gives distance 2πR and displacement 0."])
